{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_130", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('/tmp/research_history/episode9_coupling_cancellation_derivation.md')\nwith p.open('a') as f:\n f.write('''\\\\n## Exact cost-energy contraction\\\\n\\\\nThe point cost is C_ij=a_i+b_j-X_i dot R_j+L[y_i,ry_j], with a_i=||X_i||²/2 and b_j=||R_j||²/2. For q_ij=K_ij w_j, row energy is a_i sum_j q_ij + sum_j q_ij b_j - X_i dot sum_j q_ij R_j + sum_j q_ij L[y_i,ry_j]. A22-column GEMM computes mass, radial reference moment, ten coordinate moments and ten already-weighted label-cost moments together. The last ten responses are indexed by the candidate observed label. This is an exact low-rank factorization of C, not an approximation of the exponential kernel K.\\\\n\\\\nSource562 instead contracts raw reference-label indicators and multiplies each row by its ten label costs afterward;563 splits the coordinate/radial fields and label fields into two GEMMs;564 passes label-cost fields before contraction. All pass the public gates. More arithmetic can offset the avoided K*C dense memory pass, so the complete protected evaluator decides retention.\\\\n\\\\nKernel layout control: \"'`(C.T*scalar).exp2_().T` preserves the original C strides in Torch, so source555 does not create a physically column-major logical K. Sources559–561 preallocate `empty((m,n)).T` and use out= to force column-major K. Fresh processes show those true column kernels are slower. The measured source554 uses row-major K and column-shaped right operands to invoke GEMM.'\"\\\\n''')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
